BT /F1 11 Tf 72 720 Td (The Human Heart and Circulation: Study Notes) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 690 Td (1. Why the body needs a circulatory system) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 660 Td (Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients, and every cell) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 645 Td (produces waste such as carbon dioxide that must be carried away. In a tiny organism,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 630 Td (these substances can simply diffuse in and out across the surface. In a large animal) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 615 Td (like a human, most cells are far from the surface, and diffusion alone would be far too) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 600 Td (slow. The circulatory system solves this problem by pumping blood around the body) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 585 Td (through a network of tubes, so that every cell is only a short distance from a blood) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 570 Td (supply.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 540 Td (The human circulatory system has three main parts: the heart, which acts as the pump;) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 525 Td (the blood vessels, which are the tubes that carry blood; and the blood itself, which is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 510 Td (the transport medium. Together they move oxygen, glucose, hormones, antibodies and heat) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 495 Td (to where they are needed, and carry carbon dioxide, urea and other wastes to the organs) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 480 Td (that remove them.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 450 Td (2. The double circulatory system) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 420 Td (Humans have a double circulatory system. This means that blood passes through the heart) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 405 Td (twice for every complete circuit of the body. The pulmonary circuit carries blood from) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 390 Td (the heart to the lungs and back again. In the lungs, blood picks up oxygen and gets rid) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 375 Td (of carbon dioxide. The systemic circuit carries oxygenated blood from the heart to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 360 Td (rest of the body and brings deoxygenated blood back to the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 330 Td (A double circulation has an important advantage. Blood loses pressure as it passes) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 315 Td (through the tiny capillaries of the lungs. Because it returns to the heart before going) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 300 Td (to the body, the heart can pump it again and raise its pressure. This allows blood to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 285 Td (reach distant organs quickly and at high pressure, which supports the high metabolic) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 270 Td (rate of mammals. Fish, by contrast, have a single circulation, where blood passes) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 255 Td (through the gills and then flows directly on to the body at lower pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 225 Td (3. The structure of the heart) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 195 Td (The heart is a muscular organ about the size of a closed fist, found in the chest) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 180 Td (between the lungs. Its walls are made of cardiac muscle, a special type of muscle that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 165 Td (contracts rhythmically without tiring and without needing conscious control. The heart) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 150 Td (is divided into four chambers. The two upper chambers are the atria, which receive blood) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 135 Td (returning to the heart. The two lower chambers are the ventricles, which pump blood out) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 120 Td (of the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 90 Td (The right side of the heart deals with deoxygenated blood and the left side deals with) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 75 Td (oxygenated blood. A wall of muscle called the septum separates the two sides, so) Tj ET
endstream
